 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 19,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "GREEN PLAQUE: The Irish Free State issued its own pound in 1928, worth exactly a pound sterling until the link was broken in 1979. Ireland joined the euro in 1999, and euro notes and coins arrived on 1 January 2002, at 0.787564 punts to the euro. The punt stopped being legal tender that February.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": 0.475,
   "plaque": "econ_punt_euro",
   "title": "The Punt and the Euro",
   "green": true
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 22,
   "plaque": "dublin_viking",
   "title": "Dyflin",
   "text": "Viking ships wintered on the Liffey in 841, by a dark pool the Irish called Dubh Linn, and the Norse town of Dyflin grew into Ireland's busiest port. Digs at Wood Quay in the 1970s found its houses, streets and workshops. A longship built here around 1042 was found sunk in Denmark, and a replica sailed home to Dublin in 2007.",
   "people": "norse"
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 40,
   "plaque": "dublin_huguenot_cemetery",
   "title": "The Huguenot Cemetery",
   "text": "Huguenots, French Protestants driven out by persecution, came to Dublin in large numbers after 1685, bringing silk weaving, poplin and banking. Their small cemetery on Merrion Row opened in 1693. Huguenot names live on in the city: the La Touche bank, and the writers Charles Maturin and Sheridan Le Fanu.",
   "people": "huguenots"
  },
  {
   "x": 19,
   "y": 26,
   "plaque": "dublin_jewish_museum",
   "title": "Little Jerusalem",
   "text": "Jewish families fleeing persecution in the Russian Empire settled round Portobello from the 1880s, and the streets got the nickname Little Jerusalem. The Irish Jewish Museum on Walworth Road, in a former synagogue, was opened in 1985 by Chaim Herzog, who grew up in Dublin. And Joyce made Leopold Bloom, the hero of Ulysses, a Dublin Jew.",
   "people": "jewish"
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 22,
   "plaque": "dublin_famine_figures",
   "title": "The Famine Figures",
   "text": "On Custom House Quay stand Rowan Gillespie's thin bronze figures, put up in 1997, walking towards the emigrant ships. In the Great Famine of 1845 to 1852, after blight destroyed the potato crop, about a million people died and at least a million more left Ireland. The island's population has never got back to its 1840s peak.",
   "people": "irish"
  },
  {
   "text": "O'CONNELL BRIDGE. Wider than it is long. It started as a narrow stone bridge, but it was widened in 1880 to match the street, so people could say it looked square. It does.",
   "x": 34.0,
   "y": 24.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE RIVER LIFFEY. Dublin comes from 'Dubh Linn', the black pool. The Irish name is Baile Átha Cliath, 'the town of the hurdle ford'. Both names are about crossing water. Wise.",
   "x": 34.0,
   "y": 29.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE HA'PENNY BRIDGE. A cast iron footbridge from 1816 that used to cost a halfpenny to cross. The toll went in 1919. The name stuck, which is how you can tell Dubliners love a bargain.",
   "x": 21.0,
   "y": 24.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE PARK. One of the biggest walled city parks in Europe, with fallow deer that have lived here since the 1660s. The name comes from 'fionn uisce', clear water, mangled long ago into 'phoenix'.",
   "x": 16.0,
   "y": 8.0
  },
  {
   "text": "DUBLIN HALL. Where Dublin plays hurling and gets rather loud about it. It's the fastest field game in the world, played with a stick called a hurley and a ball called a sliotar. Helmets, please.",
   "x": 10.0,
   "y": 20.0
  },
  {
   "text": "TODAY ON THE STAGE: an announcement. Tote bags are free. The announcement is a surprise, and so are the tote bags.",
   "x": 47.0,
   "y": 10.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE CUSTOM HOUSE. A grand Georgian building on the quays, finished in 1791, and burned by Irish rebels in 1921. It was restored. It is quite impressive when it isn't on fire.",
   "x": 40.0,
   "y": 20.0
  },
  {
   "text": "TRINITY COLLEGE. Founded in 1592, it holds the Book of Kells, a gospel manuscript made around the year 800, and the Long Room, a library hall 65 m long with about 200,000 old books and a smell students call 'academic'.",
   "x": 11.0,
   "y": 39.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE CAMPANILE. Legend says that if you walk under it, you'll fail your exams. Students walk round it. They fail anyway, just more slowly.",
   "x": 24.0,
   "y": 44.0
  },
  {
   "text": "MOLLY MALONE. A bronze fishmonger with a barrow, from the old song, 'cockles and mussels, alive, alive-o'. She stands in a street full of buskers and has never once joined the chorus.",
   "x": 46.0,
   "y": 39.0
  },
  {
   "text": "TEMPLE BAR. Cobbled lanes, pubs, and live music from every window. The name is old, and 'bar' here means a walkway, not a pub, which no one has told the pubs.",
   "x": 35.0,
   "y": 38.0
  },
  {
   "text": "ST STEPHEN'S GREEN. 22 acres of lawn, flowerbeds and ducks, opened to the public in 1880 after Lord Ardilaun, of the Guinness family, paid for the layout. The ducks have never said thank you.",
   "x": 36.0,
   "y": 44.0
  },
  {
   "text": "DUBLIN PORT. The ferry for Holyhead leaves from here. The crossing is a little over three hours, the sea is grey, and the chips on board are optimistic.",
   "x": 60.0,
   "y": 21.0
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 14,
   "plaque": "dublin_gpo_1916",
   "title": "The GPO, 1916",
   "text": "On Easter Monday 1916 rebels took over the General Post Office on O'Connell Street, and Patrick Pearse read out the Proclamation of the Irish Republic in front of it. The Rising lasted six days. Bullet marks can still be found on the pillars."
  },
  {
   "x": 43,
   "y": 38,
   "plaque": "dublin_guinness",
   "title": "Arthur Guinness",
   "text": "In 1759 Arthur Guinness took a lease on a small brewery at St James's Gate for 9,000 years, at 45 pounds a year. The brewery is still there, and the lease is on show. The black stuff with the white head is now poured all round the world, slowly."
  },
  {
   "x": 51,
   "y": 38,
   "plaque": "dublin_bloomsday",
   "title": "Bloomsday",
   "text": "James Joyce's novel Ulysses follows Leopold Bloom round Dublin on one day, 16 June 1904, said to be the day Joyce first walked out with Nora Barnacle. Every 16 June, Dubliners dress in straw boaters, read it aloud and eat kidneys for breakfast, as Bloom did."
  },
  {
   "x": 2,
   "y": 12,
   "plaque": "dublin_lonsdale",
   "title": "Kathleen Lonsdale",
   "text": "Kathleen Lonsdale was born in 1903 in Newbridge, County Kildare, the youngest of ten children. Using X-rays, she proved in 1929 that the benzene ring is flat, which chemists had argued over for decades. In 1945 she and Marjory Stephenson became the first women elected Fellows of the Royal Society. A Quaker and a pacifist, she spent a month in Holloway prison in 1943 for refusing to register for wartime duties."
  },
  {
   "x": 9,
   "y": 37,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: Oscar Wilde, writer and wit, was born in Dublin in 1854 and studied at Trinity College from 1871 to 1874.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "dublin_oscar_wilde",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Oscar Wilde"
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 39,
   "plaque": "dublin_messiah",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Handel's Messiah",
   "text": "On 13 April 1742 Handel's Messiah was first performed at the Music Hall on Fishamble Street, near here. To fit in more people, ladies were asked not to wear hooped skirts and gentlemen to leave their swords at home."
  },
  {
   "x": 55,
   "y": 38,
   "plaque": "arts_james_joyce",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "James Joyce",
   "text": "James Joyce was born in Rathgar on 2 February 1882 and left Dublin in 1904 with Nora Barnacle, never to live here again and never to write about anywhere else. Dubliners came out in 1914, A Portrait of the Artist as a Young Man in 1916, and Ulysses on his fortieth birthday in 1922, printed in Paris by Sylvia Beach's bookshop because nobody else dared. It opens with 'Stately, plump Buck Mulligan' on a tower at Sandycove. He died in Zurich in 1941. He said that if Dublin were destroyed it could be rebuilt from his book. Planning permission would be a nightmare."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 20,
   "plaque": "arts_abbey_theatre",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "The Abbey Theatre",
   "text": "The Abbey opened on Abbey Street on 27 December 1904, founded by W. B. Yeats and Lady Gregory to give Ireland a theatre of its own. In January 1907 the audience rioted for a week at J. M. Synge's The Playboy of the Western World; Yeats called in the police and lectured the stalls. In 1925 the new Free State gave it a grant, the first state-funded theatre in the English-speaking world. The old building burned in 1951 and the present one opened in 1966. The riots have calmed down, mostly."
  },
  {
   "x": 64,
   "y": 21,
   "plaque": "arts_riverdance",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Riverdance",
   "text": "On 30 April 1994, at the Point Theatre on these docks, the Eurovision Song Contest broke for the interval and seven minutes of Irish dancing to Bill Whelan's music, led by Michael Flatley and Jean Butler, stole the whole evening. Ireland won the contest too, for the third year running, but nobody remembers the song. Riverdance opened as a full show at the Point the next February and has toured the world ever since. Arms by your sides. Feet doing the impossible."
  }
 ],
